# Module authoring rules

Read knowledge/index.md and relevant OpenSpec requirements before changing behavior.
Use OpenSpec for features, architecture, public API/CLI or configuration contract
changes, migrations, breaking upgrades, changes to recovery/state/security guarantees,
significant cross-cutting design or acceptance, and explicit user requests.
Implement routine compatible dependency/security patches, bug fixes restoring
specified behavior, refactors and docs/test/CI maintenance directly with relevant
checks and documentation. Judge actual impact; if qualifying scope emerges, plan
it before implementing it. Do not create proposals solely to log maintenance.
Add appropriate regression coverage for bug fixes. If a bug reveals a missing,
ambiguous or incorrect feature requirement, update the affected OpenSpec scenario
and requirement alongside executable tests. Restoring an already specified
contract needs no proposal; changing the intended contract follows the rules above.
For OpenSpec changes, plan, implement, verify, then sync/archive completed scope.
Keep notes in ignored .build/notes, not tracked handovers. Maintain docs, specs and
source-linked OKF knowledge together; do not invent verification provenance.

Use public Dome APIs and local go doc for the selected version. Define topology in
Go; register explicitly. Preserve non-secret identities/snapshots, real observation,
safe repeatable apply, post-apply verification and identity-only restore for pruning.
The default backend is unfinished; do not report it as production support.

Unit tests mock boundaries with Testify. Real boundary tests use integration tags;
container E2E uses Ginkgo/Gomega and Testcontainers. Run task check, task test:race,
task test:integration, task test:e2e, task check:artifacts and task docs:build for
substantial changes. Missing real dependencies fail checks; do not stub or skip.
